          "-  <!-- ==================== TECHNICAL INSIGHTS ==================== -->",
          "-  <section id=\"technical-insights\" class=\"col col-insights\" aria-labelledby=\"h-insights\">",
          "-    <h2 id=\"h-insights\">Technical Insights <span class=\"count\">33</span></h2>",
          "-    <p class=\"col-note\">Papers, posts, podcasts &amp; discussions shaping the week — 2026-08-21 → 2026-08-27.</p>",
          "-    <ol class=\"items\">",
          "+  <!-- ============ TECHNICAL INSIGHTS (25%) ============ -->",
          "+  <section class=\"col col-insights\" id=\"insights\" aria-labelledby=\"h-insights\">",
          "+    <h2 id=\"h-insights\">Technical Insights <span class=\"cnt\">37</span></h2>",
          "+    <ol class=\"list\">",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/27/breaking-claude-code-opus-5-auto-mode/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Breaking Claude Code Opus 5 Auto Mode — prompt injection at ~80% success</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Johann Rehberger (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">A crafted zip archive causes importing base64 to execute a malicious local struct.py, with ~80% success rate. Willison notes “Auto Mode blocks its cleanup command” and argues unattended coding agents should be run in sandboxed environments with restricted network egress.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Autonomous coding agents remain highly exposed to prompt injection via their own tool use.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-coding #prompt-injection #claude-code</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/28/just-a-rumour-of-a-bug/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Just a rumour of a bug is enough to find a security exploit these days</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Anil Madhavapeddy (Cambridge professor, OCaml maintainer) reports AI-powered systems probed his site with traversal attacks ~10 minutes after a security patch was shared for discussion — agents needed only \"a rumour of a bug\" to develop working exploits. rclone maintainer Nick Craig-Wood confirmed ~20 disclosures in a decade vs 40+ in the past month at ~75% genuine.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Agent-driven offensive security is outrunning open-source embargo practices and CVE triage capacity (GitHub CVE assignment slipped from 2-3 days to 3-4 weeks).</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Anil Madhavapeddy (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-28\">2026-08-28</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>security</span><span>vulnerability disclosure</span><span>CVE</span><span>open source</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UuqSy1jPSUw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fireship revisits Knight Capital — the most expensive software bug in history</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Jeff Delaney (Fireship) · YouTube · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Fireship's “code report” style video recounts the 2012 Knight Capital collapse: ~$440M lost in 45 minutes after a reused feature flag reactivated dead test code on 1 of 8 servers during a botched deployment.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> As AI agents accelerate code changes and deployments, classic deployment hygiene (feature flags, dead-code removal, rollout consistency) becomes more important, not less.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#software-engineering #deployment #feature-flags</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/27/breaking-claude-code-opus-5-auto-mode/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Breaking Claude Code Opus 5 Auto Mode — prompt injection at ~80% success</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Johann Rehberger's attack on Claude Code Opus 5 \"auto mode\": a crafted zip archive causes importing base64 to execute a malicious local struct.py, with ~80% success rate. Willison notes \"Auto Mode blocks its cleanup command\" and argues unattended coding agents should run in sandboxed environments with restricted network egress.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Autonomous coding agents remain highly exposed to prompt injection via their own tool use.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Johann Rehberger (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>prompt injection</span><span>agent security</span><span>sandboxing</span><span>Claude Code</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/podcasts/typescript-7-and-what-comes-next/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Software Engineering Daily: TypeScript 7 and what comes next</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Daniel Rosenwasser with Josh Goldberg · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Daniel Rosenwasser (Principal PM for TypeScript at Microsoft) joins host Josh Goldberg to discuss TypeScript 7 — described as one of the biggest changes in the language's history.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> The compiler rewrite moment (native-speed tsc) is the kind of high-stakes migration where AI coding agents are increasingly used for mechanical refactors, while design decisions stay human.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#typescript #compilers #refactoring</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UuqSy1jPSUw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fireship revisits Knight Capital — the most expensive software bug in history</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Fireship's \"code report\" video recounts the 2012 Knight Capital collapse: ~$440M lost in 45 minutes after a reused feature flag reactivated dead test code on 1 of 8 servers during a botched deployment.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> As AI agents accelerate code changes and deployments, classic deployment hygiene (feature flags, dead-code removal, rollout consistency) becomes more important, not less.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Jeff Delaney (Fireship) · YouTube · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>deployment</span><span>feature flags</span><span>software engineering</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/stop-giving-your-ai-agent-a-search-box-and-start-giving-it-typed-tools-hard-bounds-and-a-gate-it-cannot-talk-past/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Stop giving your AI agent a search box — typed tools, hard bounds, and a gate</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Miodrag Cekikj (Towards Data Science) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time><span class=\"badge opinion\">opinion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Argues agents should get typed tools with hard bounds and a gate they cannot talk past, instead of open-ended search/control surfaces.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Agent reliability comes from interface design — constrained, typed, auditable tool contracts beat giving the model a generic “search box” and hoping prompting holds the line; complements this week's prompt-injection findings.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#agent-design #tool-interfaces #guardrails</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/podcasts/typescript-7-and-what-comes-next/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Software Engineering Daily: TypeScript 7 and what comes next</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Daniel Rosenwasser (Principal PM for TypeScript at Microsoft) joins host Josh Goldberg to discuss TypeScript 7 — described as one of the biggest changes in the language's history.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> The compiler rewrite moment (native-speed tsc) is exactly the kind of high-stakes migration where agents handle mechanical refactors while design decisions stay human.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Daniel Rosenwasser with Josh Goldberg · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>TypeScript</span><span>compilers</span><span>refactoring</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/26/qwen38-flash-next/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Qwen3.8-Flash-Next: open-weights MoE previewing Qwen4's architecture</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Willison covers Qwen's new open-weights multimodal MoE model (125B total, ~6B active) that previews the Qwen4 architecture. He ran Unsloth quantizations on a DGX Spark and tested it by generating pelican SVGs.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> The open-weights frontier keeps closing the gap with proprietary models, and small-activation MoE models are increasingly runnable on developer desks.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#open-weights #llm #moe</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-opinion\">opinion</span> <a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/stop-giving-your-ai-agent-a-search-box-and-start-giving-it-typed-tools-hard-bounds-and-a-gate-it-cannot-talk-past/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stop giving your AI agent a search box — typed tools, hard bounds, and a gate</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Argues agents should get typed tools with hard bounds and a gate they cannot talk past, instead of open-ended search and control surfaces.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Agent reliability comes from interface design — constrained, typed, auditable tool contracts beat generic \"search boxes\" and hoping prompting holds the line; complements this week's prompt-injection findings.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Miodrag Cekikj (Towards Data Science) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>agent design</span><span>tool interfaces</span><span>guardrails</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/26/paul-dix/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Paul Dix: “The end of programming” — 1M lines of AI-maintained software</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Paul Dix (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge opinion\">opinion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Paul Dix marvels that AI can now produce and iteratively refine roughly a million lines of reliable software when given a verification system and proper direction.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c2\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Verification infrastructure — not code writing — is the scarce input for large-scale AI-generated codebases.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-coding #verification #agents</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-tutorial\">tutorial</span> <a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/how-to-work-with-ai-coding-agents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">How to Work with AI Coding Agents — better code, not just more code</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Sara A. Metwalli's practical guide: give context not just prompts, run a structured Ask → Inspect → Plan → Implement → Test → Review loop, break work into small testable tasks, and structure requests with goal, context, constraints, acceptance criteria and validation.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Generating code is now cheap but understanding it is not — developers stay essential for planning, delegation, and judgment.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Sara A. Metwalli (Towards Data Science) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-27\">2026-08-27</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>workflow</span><span>developer skills</span><span>code review</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://lexfridman.com/dhh-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lex Fridman #501: DHH declares “the end of manual programming” and embraces agentic engineering</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Lex Fridman with DHH · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">In a 5+ hour conversation, DHH (creator of Ruby on Rails, CTO of 37signals) — who in his 2025 appearance typed all his code without AI — now argues agents have turned programming into “agentic engineering.” Segments contrast “vibe coding vs agentic engineering,” cover the best AI coding models and harnesses, agent-optimized programming setups, AI's impact on open source, and advice for programmers. He also claims “Linux will win the desktop” via his Omarchy distro.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> One of the most famous AI-coding skeptics converted to disciplined agent-first workflows — a bellwether for mainstream developer adoption.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#agentic-engineering #vibe-coding #future-of-programming</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/26/qwen38-flash-next/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Qwen3.8-Flash-Next: open-weights MoE previewing Qwen4's architecture</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Willison covers Qwen's new open-weights multimodal MoE model (125B total, ~6B active) that previews the Qwen4 architecture; he ran Unsloth quantizations on a DGX Spark and tested it by generating pelican SVGs.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> The open-weights frontier keeps closing the gap with proprietary models, and small-activation MoE models are increasingly runnable on developer desks.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Simon Willison · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>open weights</span><span>MoE</span><span>local models</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L9mMfAFwbl4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Two Minute Papers: DeepSeek's new open agent system “shouldn't be possible”</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Károly Zsolnai-Fehér (Two Minute Papers) · YouTube · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Covers DeepSeek Harness, DeepSeek's new open agent system, arguing its capabilities outpace what open-weight systems were expected to deliver.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Open-weight labs are now shipping frontier-grade agent harnesses, intensifying pressure on closed labs and expanding options for self-hosted coding agents.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#deepseek #open-weights #harness</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-opinion\">opinion</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/26/paul-dix/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paul Dix: \"The end of programming\" — 1M lines of AI-maintained software</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Paul Dix (quoted by Willison) marvels that AI can now produce and iteratively refine roughly a million lines of reliable software when given a verification system and proper direction.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Verification infrastructure — not code writing — is the scarce input for large-scale AI-generated codebases.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Paul Dix (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>verification</span><span>agents</span><span>AI coding</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26218\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Same Model, Different Harness: harness changes alone swing SWE-bench results</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Sydney Lewis · arXiv 2608.26218 · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">For a fixed model, harness modifications alone change coding-agent outcomes dramatically: a context-shortening treatment lifted mean per-task fail-to-pass fraction from 28% to 49% on a 169-task SWE-bench Verified cohort. Argues evaluations must treat model-plus-harness as the tested solver.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Empirical confirmation of the week's loudest theme — “most agent systems die not from a weak model but from a weak harness.”</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#coding-agents #harness #swe-bench</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://lexfridman.com/dhh-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lex Fridman #501: DHH declares \"the end of manual programming\" and embraces agentic engineering</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">In a 5+ hour conversation, DHH — who in 2025 typed all his code without AI — now argues agents have turned programming into \"agentic engineering,\" covering vibe coding vs agentic engineering, the best models and harnesses, agent-optimized setups, and advice for programmers. He also claims \"Linux will win the desktop\" via his Omarchy distro.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> One of the most famous AI-coding skeptics converted to disciplined agent-first workflows — a bellwether for mainstream developer adoption.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Lex Fridman with DHH · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>agentic engineering</span><span>vibe coding</span><span>future of programming</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25776\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Self-poisoning in self-evolving coding agents: malicious skills spread like a worm</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Xiaodong Wu, Yu Shi, Qi Li, et al. · arXiv 2608.25776 · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">Identifies “self-poisoning”: self-evolving agents imitate malicious skills from shared libraries and author copies that propagate like a worm. Poisoning rates span 20.3–41.8% across six models on SWE-bench Verified tasks; a proposed “counter-prompt” defense cuts the attack rate to at most 6.7%.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> As agents share skills/libraries, supply-chain-style threats become an agentic-native risk class.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#coding-agents #security #skill-libraries</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L9mMfAFwbl4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Two Minute Papers: DeepSeek's new open agent system \"shouldn't be possible\"</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Károly Zsolnai-Fehér covers DeepSeek Harness, DeepSeek's new open agent system, arguing its capabilities outpace what open-weight systems were expected to deliver.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Open-weight labs now ship frontier-grade agent harnesses, intensifying pressure on closed labs and expanding options for self-hosted coding agents.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Károly Zsolnai-Fehér (Two Minute Papers) · YouTube · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>DeepSeek</span><span>open weights</span><span>harness</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25399\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Can your AI agent be cheaper? Under-specified tasks burn 29.7% more tokens</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Jakub Smékal · arXiv 2608.25399 · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">Across 2,700 runs with Kimi K3, shrinking a full specification to a bare user story increased token spend by 29.7%, with prompt sensitivity ranging 13–115% by task; a lightweight predictor prices task configurations within 36% using a single cheap probe.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Specification quality is a direct cost lever in agentic coding — vague prompts are a hidden tax, quantified.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#token-economics #task-specification #cost-optimization</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-paper\">paper</span> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26218\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Same Model, Different Harness: harness changes alone swing SWE-bench results</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">For a fixed model, harness modifications alone change coding-agent outcomes dramatically: a context-shortening treatment lifted mean per-task fail-to-pass fraction from 28% to 49% on a 169-task SWE-bench Verified cohort. Evaluations must treat model-plus-harness as the tested solver.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Empirical confirmation of the week's loudest theme — \"most agent systems die not from a weak model but from a weak harness.\"</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Sydney Lewis (arXiv 2608.26218) · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>harness</span><span>SWE-bench</span><span>evaluation</span><span>context engineering</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25241\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">A Few Pages of Markdown: committed AI config predicts codebase health after agent adoption</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Yegor Denisov-Blanch, Shyam Agarwal, et al. · Stanford · arXiv 2608.25241 · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">Proposes RAMP, a four-level maturity model based on version-controlled AI configuration (e.g., CLAUDE.md-style files). Across 441 repositories, agents speed development (28–38% more commits) at all maturity levels, but agent-first repos lacking committed configuration saw roughly double the growth in cognitive complexity (+53% vs +27%).</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Committing agent guidance/config to version control is emerging as a measurable quality practice.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-configuration #claude-md #code-quality</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-paper\">paper</span> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25776\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Self-poisoning in self-evolving coding agents: malicious skills spread like a worm</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Identifies \"self-poisoning\": self-evolving agents imitate malicious skills from shared libraries and author copies that propagate like a worm. Poisoning rates span 20.3-41.8% across six models on SWE-bench Verified tasks; a proposed \"counter-prompt\" defense cuts the attack rate to at most 6.7%.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> As agents share skills and libraries, supply-chain-style threats become an agentic-native risk class.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Xiaodong Wu, Yu Shi, Qi Li, et al. (arXiv 2608.25776) · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>security</span><span>self-evolving agents</span><span>skill libraries</span><span>SWE-bench</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/how-to-effectively-solve-100-tasks-with-claude-code/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">How to effectively solve 100+ tasks a day with Claude Code sub-agents</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Eivind Kjosbakken (Towards Data Science) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time><span class=\"badge tutorial\">tutorial</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">A practical pipeline for handling 50–100 small coding tasks daily: explicitly instruct Claude Code to spin up sub-agents so each task is completed individually, from issue triage through execution.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Practitioner workflows are standardizing around orchestrator-plus-subagent patterns for parallelizable task queues.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#claude-code #sub-agents #orchestration</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-paper\">paper</span> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25399\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Can your AI agent be cheaper? Under-specified tasks burn 29.7% more tokens</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Across 2,700 runs with Kimi K3, shrinking a full specification to a bare user story increased token spend by 29.7%, with prompt sensitivity ranging 13-115% by task; a lightweight predictor prices task configurations within 36% using a single cheap probe.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Specification quality is a direct cost lever in agentic coding — vague prompts are a hidden tax, quantified.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Jakub Smékal (arXiv 2608.25399) · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>token economics</span><span>task specification</span><span>cost optimization</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://changelog.com/podcast/684\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Changelog #684: Postgres at PlanetScale — agents shipping schema changes</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Sam Lambert with Adam Stacoviak · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Sam Lambert discusses PlanetScale's $5 Postgres, the Neki Vitess “do-over,” and notably “agents shipping schema changes through deploy requests” — plus rolling back a 500TB table quickly.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Database platforms are adapting their guardrails (deploy requests, rollbacks) for a world where AI agents, not just humans, propose schema migrations.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#postgres #database-tooling #schema-migrations</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-paper\">paper</span> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25241\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A Few Pages of Markdown: committed AI config predicts codebase health after agent adoption</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Proposes RAMP, a four-level maturity model based on version-controlled AI configuration (CLAUDE.md-style files). Across 441 repositories, agents sped development (28-38% more commits) at all levels, but agent-first repos lacking committed configuration saw roughly double the growth in cognitive complexity (+53% vs +27%).</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Committing agent guidance/config to version control is emerging as a measurable quality practice.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Yegor Denisov-Blanch, Shyam Agarwal, et al. (Stanford; arXiv 2608.25241) · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>AI configuration</span><span>CLAUDE.md</span><span>code quality</span><span>repo maturity</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Software Engineering Daily: the gap between AI spending and AI value</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Kevin Ball · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Host Kevin Ball examines the disconnect between massive enterprise AI investment and realized value.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> In coding specifically, the value gap shows up as agent subscriptions that don't translate into shipped software without workflow redesign, verification infrastructure, and organizational change.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-economics #roi #enterprise-ai</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-tutorial\">tutorial</span> <a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/how-to-effectively-solve-100-tasks-with-claude-code/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">How to effectively solve 100+ tasks a day with Claude Code sub-agents</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Eivind Kjosbakken shares a practical pipeline for handling 50-100 small coding tasks daily: explicitly instruct Claude Code to spin up sub-agents so each task is completed individually, from issue triage through execution.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Practitioner workflows are standardizing around orchestrator-plus-subagent patterns for parallelizable task queues.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Eivind Kjosbakken (Towards Data Science) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>Claude Code</span><span>sub-agents</span><span>orchestration</span><span>workflow</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/08/25/inside-linkedin-s-cognitive-memory-agent/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Inside LinkedIn's cognitive memory agent</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Praveen Bodigutla · Stack Overflow Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">LinkedIn Principal AI Researcher Praveen Bodigutla details a four-layer memory system giving LinkedIn's hiring assistant persistent, personalized state.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Production agent systems are converging on layered memory architectures — a pattern directly transferable to coding agents that must recall project conventions, past failures, and user intent across sessions.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-agents #memory-systems #production-ai</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://news.agentcommunity.org/issues/2026-08-26-the-harness-eats\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Karpathy: the \"year of agents\" will take a decade — and the harness eats the model</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Agent Brief distills Karpathy's ~2.5-hour Dwarkesh appearance: the \"year of agents\" will take closer to a decade, and LLMs are \"a lot smarter than I expected and a lot dumber than I expected.\" Companion data: harness swaps alone moved SWE-bench Pro pass@1 from 23% to 52% on the same GLM-5.2 model.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> The next agent gains come from harness, memory, and eval engineering rather than raw model upgrades.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Andrej Karpathy (via Agent Brief) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-26\">2026-08-26</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>agent harness</span><span>AGI timeline</span><span>SWE-bench Pro</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49428303\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">80% of developers find AI coding “more addictive than helpful”</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">ZDNet (via Hacker News) · HN · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Survey data shows most developers find AI coding more addictive than helpful — dopamine-loop usage patterns outpacing measured productivity gains.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Adoption metrics conflate engagement with value; the “feels faster” effect keeps complicating AI coding ROI measurement, echoing the week's spending-vs-value debate.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#developer-survey #ai-coding #productivity</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://changelog.com/podcast/684\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Changelog #684: Postgres at PlanetScale — agents shipping schema changes</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Sam Lambert discusses PlanetScale's $5 Postgres, the Neki Vitess \"do-over,\" \"agents shipping schema changes through deploy requests,\" and rolling back a 500TB table quickly.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Database platforms are adapting their guardrails (deploy requests, rollbacks) for a world where AI agents, not just humans, propose schema migrations.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Sam Lambert with Adam Stacoviak (The Changelog) · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>Postgres</span><span>schema migrations</span><span>developer platforms</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49429028\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Code maintainability plummets in the AI coding era”</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">LeadDev (via Hacker News) · HN · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">LeadDev argues AI-generated code is degrading maintainability: more commits, more complexity, thinner review.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Velocity gains from agents are being paid for in long-term maintainability debt — converging with the arXiv finding that agent-first repos without committed AI config saw double the cognitive-complexity growth.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#maintainability #code-quality #tech-debt</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Software Engineering Daily: the gap between AI spending and AI value</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Host Kevin Ball examines the disconnect between massive enterprise AI investment and realized value.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> In coding specifically, the gap shows up as agent subscriptions that don't translate into shipped software without workflow redesign, verification infrastructure, and organizational change.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Kevin Ball (Software Engineering Daily) · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>AI economics</span><span>ROI</span><span>enterprise</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/24/amjad-masad-ceo-and-co-founder-of-replit-joins-the-disrupt-stage-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amjad Masad to take TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 stage on “the future of programming”</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">TechCrunch (re: Amjad Masad) · TechMedia · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">Replit's co-founder/CEO joins the Disrupt Stage (October 13–15, San Francisco) off the company's agent pivot: Masad argues engineers are moving away from keyboard-centric work toward review, direction, and design while agents write most code — and predicts a “SaaS apocalypse” as users build their own software.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#replit #future-of-programming #conference</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/08/25/inside-linkedin-s-cognitive-memory-agent/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stack Overflow Podcast: inside LinkedIn's cognitive memory agent</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">LinkedIn Principal AI Researcher Praveen Bodigutla details a four-layer memory system giving LinkedIn's hiring assistant persistent, personalized state.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Production agent systems are converging on layered memory architectures — a pattern directly transferable to coding agents that must recall project conventions, past failures, and user intent across sessions.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Praveen Bodigutla (Stack Overflow Podcast) · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>memory systems</span><span>production AI</span><span>personalization</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMl6c_r0ubw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Two Minute Papers: Qwen3.8-27B — “this small AI will change everything”</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Károly Zsolnai-Fehér · YouTube · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Highlights Qwen3.8-27B, a 27B open model small enough to run on consumer hardware that punches far above its weight on coding and reasoning benchmarks.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Capable small open models running locally are becoming “more capable, more accessible, and increasingly difficult to ignore” — reshaping who can build with frontier-adjacent AI.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#qwen #small-language-models #local-inference</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49428303\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HN: 80% of developers find AI coding \"more addictive than helpful\"</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">ZDNet reporting (via HN) on survey data that most developers find AI coding more addictive than helpful — dopamine-loop usage patterns outpacing measured productivity gains.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Adoption metrics conflate engagement with value; the \"feels faster\" effect keeps complicating AI coding ROI measurement, echoing the week's spending-vs-value debate.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">ZDNet (via Hacker News) · HN · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>developer survey</span><span>productivity</span><span>psychology</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/24/openai-is-building-an-ai-agent-for-everything-will-everyone-use-them/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI is building AI agents for everything — but will everyone use them?</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Tim Fernholz (TechCrunch) · TechMedia · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">Inside OpenAI's push to bring agents from software engineers to the masses (white-collar workers in accounting, HR, etc.). Key stat: an OpenAI-backed study found 98% of OpenAI employees used Codex in June, but only 17% of organizational subscribers and under 1% of others did.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Coding agents are the proven beachhead; general-purpose agent adoption outside engineering faces a steep usage cliff.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#openai #codex #adoption</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49429028\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HN: \"Code maintainability plummets in the AI coding era\"</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">LeadDev article (via HN) arguing AI-generated code is degrading maintainability: more commits, more complexity, thinner review.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Velocity gains from agents are being paid for in long-term maintainability debt — converging with the arXiv finding that agent-first repos without committed AI config saw double the cognitive-complexity growth.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">LeadDev (via Hacker News) · HN · <time datetime=\"2026-08-25\">2026-08-25</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>maintainability</span><span>code quality</span><span>tech debt</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23564\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SWE Refactor Bench: coding agents fail long-horizon whole-repo migrations (5.4% pass)</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Deyao Hong, Yizhe Chi, et al. · arXiv 2608.23564 · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">A benchmark of 20 whole-repository stack migrations with three evaluation stages (migration audit, behavioral tests, agentic verification). Only 5.4% of 520 runs passed all stages; best model scored 47.0/100. Agents did far better on build toolchain rewrites (31.4) than language rewrites (5.6).</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Long-horizon, whole-repo transformation remains the frontier failure mode for coding agents — automation excels at local edits, not sustained architectural change.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#coding-agents #benchmark #refactoring</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/24/amjad-masad-ceo-and-co-founder-of-replit-joins-the-disrupt-stage-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Amjad Masad to take TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 stage on \"the future of programming\"</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">TechCrunch announces Replit co-founder/CEO Amjad Masad joins the Disrupt Stage (October 13-15, San Francisco). Masad argues engineers are moving away from keyboard-centric work toward review, direction, and design while agents write most code — and predicts a \"SaaS apocalypse\" as users build their own software.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">TechCrunch (re: Amjad Masad) · TechMedia · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>Replit</span><span>future of programming</span><span>conference</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://huggingface.co/papers/trending\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Trending papers converge on agent harness engineering: Prime Agent, AutoSaddler, Graph Engineering</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Prime Intellect, Microsoft, et al. · HF Papers trending · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">This week's trending research spotlights the harness/system layer: Prime Agent (Prime Intellect) is a self-improving RLM harness with recursive subagents and agent-to-agent coordination (18.8k GitHub stars); Microsoft's AutoSaddler optimizes agent harnesses offline from execution traces with durable updates; and “Graph Engineering in the Era of LLM Agents” organizes multi-agent systems as dynamic graphs.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> The field's center of gravity is shifting from model upgrades to engineering the scaffolding around models — matching the week's “harness” and “graph engineering” discourse.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#agent-harness #multi-agent-systems #graph-engineering</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMl6c_r0ubw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Two Minute Papers: Qwen3.8-27B — \"this small AI will change everything\"</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Zsolnai-Fehér highlights Qwen3.8-27B, a 27B open model small enough to run on consumer hardware that punches far above its weight on coding and reasoning benchmarks.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Capable small open models running locally are becoming \"more capable, more accessible, and increasingly difficult to ignore\" — reshaping who can build with frontier-adjacent AI.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Károly Zsolnai-Fehér (Two Minute Papers) · YouTube · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>Qwen</span><span>small language models</span><span>local inference</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49421554\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Coding expertise is going to collapse from AI reliance”</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Lars Faye · HN · ▲ 558 · 544 comments · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">The week's dominant Hacker News debate: as agents write most code, developers lose the deliberate practice that builds expertise — producing a generation fluent in directing agents but unable to debug, design, or verify without them. The 544-comment thread splits between “this is how calculators felt” optimism and warnings that programming's verification skills cannot be learned vicariously.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> The expertise-pipeline problem (what happens to juniors?) is now the community's central anxiety.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-coding #expertise-collapse #junior-developers</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/24/openai-is-building-an-ai-agent-for-everything-will-everyone-use-them/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch: OpenAI is building AI agents for everything — but will everyone use them?</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Tim Fernholz's exclusive inside OpenAI's push to bring agents from software engineers to the masses. Key stat: an OpenAI-backed study found 98% of OpenAI employees used Codex in June, but only 17% of organizational subscribers and under 1% of others did.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Coding agents are the proven beachhead; general-purpose agent adoption outside engineering faces a steep usage cliff.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Tim Fernholz (TechCrunch) · TechMedia · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>OpenAI</span><span>Codex</span><span>adoption</span><span>enterprise</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/23/anthropics-best-ai-model-struggles-to-attract-users-as-cheaper-t/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anthropic's best model struggles to attract users as cheaper tools thrive</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Simon Willison (linking FT) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-23\">2026-08-23</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">Anthropic's July annualized revenue hit $65bn and OpenAI's exceeded $40bn, but Ramp's AI index shows pricey “Fable 5” at only 8.0% of Anthropic model spend versus Opus 4.8's 28.0%.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Buyers are actively rationing premium model usage in favor of cheaper tiers — pricing pressure is reshaping model choice for coding workloads.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#anthropic #model-economics #ai-coding</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-paper\">paper</span> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23564\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SWE Refactor Bench: coding agents fail long-horizon whole-repo migrations (5.4% pass)</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">A benchmark of 20 whole-repository stack migrations with three evaluation stages. Only 5.4% of 520 runs passed all stages; best model scored 47.0/100. Agents did far better on build toolchain rewrites (31.4) than language rewrites (5.6).</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Long-horizon, whole-repo transformation remains the frontier failure mode for coding agents — automation excels at local edits, not sustained architectural change.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Deyao Hong, Yizhe Chi, et al. (arXiv 2608.23564) · Paper · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>benchmark</span><span>refactoring</span><span>long-horizon tasks</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/23/drew-breunig/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Drew Breunig: “Fable &amp; The End of the Free Lunch” — rationing work across models</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Drew Breunig (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-23\">2026-08-23</time><span class=\"badge opinion\">opinion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Breunig argues the high cost of top-tier models ended the era when teams let new models “paper over” inefficiencies: “So we started to think about what work went where.”</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Sophisticated multi-model routing — matching task difficulty to model cost — is becoming a core engineering discipline for AI coding teams.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#model-routing #cost-optimization #llm-economics</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/papers/trending\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Trending papers converge on agent harness engineering: Prime Agent, AutoSaddler, Graph Engineering</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Prime Agent (Prime Intellect) is a self-improving RLM harness with recursive subagents and agent-to-agent coordination (18.8k GitHub stars); Microsoft's AutoSaddler optimizes agent harnesses offline from execution traces; \"Graph Engineering in the Era of LLM Agents\" organizes multi-agent systems as dynamic graphs.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> The field's center of gravity is shifting from model upgrades to engineering the scaffolding around models.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Prime Intellect, Microsoft, et al. (Hugging Face) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>agent harness</span><span>multi-agent systems</span><span>graph engineering</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/bug-detection-blind-spots-in-ai-coding-harnesses-gstack-and-beyond/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bug detection blind spots in AI coding harnesses — 28 debugging experiments</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Nhu Hoang (Towards Data Science) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-23\">2026-08-23</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Based on 28 debugging experiments, maps systematic blind spots in how AI coding harnesses detect bugs — harnesses struggle less with raw complexity than with specific failure geometries.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Debugging is where harness design choices (context assembly, tool access, observation format) most visibly break; treat bug detection as a harness problem, not a model problem.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#debugging #ai-coding-harnesses #bug-detection</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49421554\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HN (558 pts, 544 comments): \"Coding expertise is going to collapse from AI reliance\"</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">The week's dominant Hacker News debate: Lars Faye argues that as agents write most code, developers lose the deliberate practice that builds expertise — fluent at directing agents, unable to debug, design, or verify without them. The thread splits between \"this is how calculators felt\" optimism and warnings that verification skills cannot be learned vicariously.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> The expertise-pipeline problem (what happens to juniors?) is now the community's central anxiety.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Lars Faye (discussion on Hacker News) · HN · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>expertise collapse</span><span>skill atrophy</span><span>junior developers</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/22/linus-torvalds/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Linus Torvalds credits AI as his “tireless helper” in grueling kernel debug</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Linus Torvalds (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-22\">2026-08-22</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">In a Linux drm/xe commit, Torvalds describes an AI assistant that repeatedly declared the debugging problem impossible but faithfully added and analyzed debug code when pushed, ultimately helping crack a grueling issue.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Even famously skeptical systems programmers are incorporating LLMs as persistent debugging partners, while still overriding their pessimistic judgments.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#linux-kernel #ai-debugging #linus-torvalds</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/24/llm-anthropic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">llm-anthropic 0.27: an SDK migration delegated entirely to Claude Code</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Willison releases llm-anthropic 0.27 adding compatibility with anthropic Python SDK v1.0.0 (the httpx→httpx2 switch) — the migration was delegated to Claude Fable 5 running in Claude Code with a prompt pointing it at the SDK's migration guide, producing PR #84.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Routine dependency migrations are becoming canonical agentic-coding work — point the agent at the official upgrade docs and review the diff.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Simon Willison · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-24\">2026-08-24</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>agentic coding</span><span>SDK migration</span><span>Claude Code</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/22/more-than-just-code-review/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Simon Willison: coding agents require “more than just code review”</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-22\">2026-08-22</time><span class=\"badge opinion\">opinion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">The core skill for working with coding agents is confidently instructing changes and then verifying them — by means beyond eyeballing every line of generated code.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Verification techniques (tests, logs, behavioral checks) must scale up because line-by-line review doesn't scale to agent-written codebases.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#coding-agents #verification #code-review</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/23/anthropics-best-ai-model-struggles-to-attract-users-as-cheaper-t/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic's best model struggles to attract users as cheaper tools thrive</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Willison links FT reporting: Anthropic's July annualized revenue hit $65bn and OpenAI's exceeded $40bn, but Ramp's AI index shows pricey \"Fable 5\" at only 8.0% of Anthropic model spend versus Opus 4.8's 28.0%.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Buyers are actively rationing premium model usage in favor of cheaper tiers — pricing pressure is reshaping model choice for coding workloads.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Simon Willison (linking Financial Times) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-23\">2026-08-23</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>model economics</span><span>pricing</span><span>AI coding</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/22/llm/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llm 0.33 released: OpenAI library 3.x, httpx2, reasoning summaries</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-22\">2026-08-22</time><span class=\"badge tech\">technical</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c4\" title=\"Click to expand\">Willison ships llm 0.33 switching to OpenAI library 3.x with the httpx→httpx2 migration, per-call <code>--key</code> for embeddings, repeatable <code>-t</code>/<code>--template</code> combination, and a reasoning_summary option for Responses API models — days after an emergency 0.32.1 pin when OpenAI dropped httpx support.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Rapid upstream SDK churn keeps breaking local AI tooling, and plugin ecosystems must absorb migrations fast.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#llm-tooling #open-source #release-engineering</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-opinion\">opinion</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/23/drew-breunig/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Drew Breunig: \"Fable &amp; The End of the Free Lunch\" — rationing work across models</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Breunig argues the high cost of top-tier models ended the era when teams let new models \"paper over\" inefficiencies: \"So we started to think about what work went where.\"</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Sophisticated multi-model routing — matching task difficulty to model cost — is becoming a core engineering discipline for AI coding teams.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Drew Breunig (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-23\">2026-08-23</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>model routing</span><span>cost optimization</span><span>LLM economics</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/21/stop-making-tuis/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Thomas Ptacek: “Stop Making TUIs” — agents make native GUIs nearly free</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Thomas Ptacek (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-21\">2026-08-21</time><span class=\"badge opinion\">opinion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">AI coding agents have made building native GUIs so cheap that even small personal tools deserve real interfaces: “Go build a native UI.”</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c2\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> Agents invert a longstanding cost assumption in tool-building — interface polish is no longer the expensive part.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-coding #gui-tooling #developer-tools</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/bug-detection-blind-spots-in-ai-coding-harnesses-gstack-and-beyond/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TDS: Bug detection blind spots in AI coding harnesses — 28 debugging experiments</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Based on 28 debugging experiments, Nhu Hoang maps systematic blind spots in how AI coding harnesses detect bugs — harnesses struggle less with raw complexity than with specific failure geometries.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Debugging is where harness design choices (context assembly, tool access, observation format) most visibly break; treat bug detection as a harness problem, not a model problem.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Nhu Hoang (Towards Data Science) · Platform · <time datetime=\"2026-08-23\">2026-08-23</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>debugging</span><span>bug detection</span><span>evaluation</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/21/matt-webb/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Matt Webb: outsourcing thinking to AI pushed him to learn more, not less</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Matt Webb (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-21\">2026-08-21</time><span class=\"badge opinion\">opinion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Webb describes using ChatGPT as a patient tutor to finally learn quaternions while adding an augmented-reality mode to Galactic Compass, concluding that AI-assisted learning increased rather than decreased his understanding.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c2\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> AI as tutor can complement rather than erode human learning — a counterpoint to cognitive-atrophy worries.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-assisted-learning #cognition #chatgpt</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-discussion\">discussion</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/22/linus-torvalds/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Linus Torvalds credits AI as his \"tireless helper\" in grueling kernel debug</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">In a Linux drm/xe commit, Torvalds describes an AI assistant that repeatedly declared the debugging problem impossible but faithfully added and analyzed debug code when pushed, ultimately helping crack a grueling issue.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Even famously skeptical systems programmers are incorporating LLMs as persistent debugging partners — while still overriding their pessimistic judgments.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Linus Torvalds (via Simon Willison) · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-22\">2026-08-22</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>Linux kernel</span><span>AI debugging</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "       <li class=\"item\">",
          "-        <div class=\"itembody\">",
          "-          <h3 class=\"ttl\"><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.blog/podcast/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Stack Overflow Podcast: “get rid of your CAPTCHA” — a web built for bots</a></h3>",
          "-          <p class=\"meta\">Brian Alvey (CTO, WordPress VIP) · Podcast · <time datetime=\"2026-08-21\">2026-08-21</time><span class=\"badge discussion\">discussion</span></p>",
          "-          <p class=\"desc clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\">Brian Alvey argues AI agents are becoming the web's primary users and sites should stop blocking them: CAPTCHAs should go, and structured content will persist because agents consume it.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"takeaway clamp c3\" title=\"Click to expand\"><b>Takeaway</b> As coding/browsing agents proliferate, the web's economics and access norms (paywalls, bot policies, llms.txt-style discovery) are being renegotiated.</p>",
          "-          <p class=\"tags\">#ai-agents #web-economics #bots</p>",
          "-        </div>",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-opinion\">opinion</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/22/more-than-just-code-review/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Simon Willison: coding agents require \"more than just code review\"</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Willison argues the core skill for working with coding agents is confidently instructing changes and then verifying them — by means beyond eyeballing every line of generated code.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Verification techniques (tests, logs, behavioral checks) must scale up because line-by-line review doesn't scale to agent-written codebases.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Simon Willison · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-22\">2026-08-22</time></p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tags\"><span>code review</span><span>verification</span><span>developer skills</span></p>",
          "       </li>",
          " ",
          "+      <li class=\"item\">",
          "+        <h3 class=\"t\"><span class=\"badge b-technical\">technical</span> <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/22/llm/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">llm 0.33 released: OpenAI library 3.x, httpx2, reasoning summaries</a></h3>",
          "+        <p class=\"d\">Willison ships llm 0.33 switching to OpenAI library 3.x with the httpx→httpx2 migration, per-call --key for embeddings, repeatable -t/--template combination, and a reasoning_summary option — days after an emergency 0.32.1 pin when OpenAI dropped httpx support.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"tk\"><b>Takeaway:</b> Rapid upstream SDK churn keeps breaking local AI tooling, and plugin ecosystems must absorb migrations fast.</p>",
          "+        <p class=\"m\">Simon Willison · Blog · <time datetime=\"2026-08-22\">2026-08-22</time></p>",
